================================================================================
WHO CALLS cpsw_rx_handler() AND WHEN?
================================================================================

1. cpsw_rx_handler() is registered as the RX completion callback when the CPSW
   (TI Ethernet Switch) driver creates RX DMA channels during the probe() stage.

2. During probe(), the CPSW driver calls:
       cpdma_chan_create()
   and passes cpsw_rx_handler() as the RX callback function.

3. When a packet is received by hardware:
   - DMA writes the packet into one of the page buffers provided by the driver.
   - DMA completes the descriptor.
   - The CPDMA engine generates an interrupt.
   - The interrupt handler wakes NAPI polling or directly calls the RX callback.
   - The CPDMA driver calls:
         cpsw_rx_handler(token, len, status)

4. The parameters passed:
   - token = page pointer the driver supplied earlier during descriptor setup
   - len   = actual received packet length
   - status= CPDMA status bits (success, errors, VLAN info, port source, etc.)

This function is called ON EVERY RECEIVED PACKET, in interrupt/NAPI context.


================================================================================
OVERVIEW OF WHAT THE FUNCTION DOES (FULL, NO INFO OMITTED)
================================================================================

1. Extract the page buffer and access its memory.
2. Read CPSW metadata stored inside that page (ndev + channel).
3. Identify the correct network device (in dual-EMAC mode).
4. Validate status; if the interface is down, recycle or requeue the buffer.
5. Allocate a new RX page so the current one can be used for SKB/XDP.
6. If XDP program is attached:
      - Prepare xdp_buff structure
      - Handle VLAN encapsulation offsets
      - Execute XDP program
      - If XDP returns DROP/REDIRECT/ABORT → requeue this page to DMA
7. If XDP passes the packet:
      - Convert the page to an SKB (build_skb)
      - Reserve headroom
      - Copy packet length
      - Handle VLAN tag extraction if needed
      - Add hardware RX timestamp (if enabled)
      - Run eth_type_trans() to determine L3 protocol
8. Release the page back to the pool for normal stack SKB operation.
9. Deliver the SKB to Linux networking stack with netif_receive_skb().
10. Update stats: rx_bytes and rx_packets.
11. REQUEUE BLOCK:
      - Write metadata (ndev + channel) into new page
      - Get DMA-mapped address for the page
      - Submit page as a fresh RX descriptor to DMA engine
      - If submission fails, recycle page back to pool.


